d Sánchez-Puertas, P. and Signer, A. and Sitison, J. W. and Stamen, D. and Stöckinger, D. and Stöckinger-Kim, H. and Stoffer, P. and Sue, Y. and Tavella, P. and Teubner, T. and Toelstede, J.-N. and Toledo, G. and Bobadilla, W. J. Torres and Tsang, J. T. and Ucci, F. P. and Ulrich, Y. and family=Water, given=R. S. Van, prefix=de, useprefix=false and Venanzoni, G. and Volkov, S. and family=Hippel, given=G., prefix=von, useprefix=false and Wang, G. and Wenger, U. and Wittig, H. and Wright, A. and Zaid, E. and Zanke, M. and Zhang, Z. and Zillinger, M. and Alexandrou, C. and Altherr, A. and Anderson, M. and Aubin, C. and Bacchio, S. and Beltrame, P. and Beltran, A. and Boyle, P. and Plasencia, I. Campos and Caprini, I. and Chakraborty, B. and Chanturia, G. and Crivellin, A. and Czarnecki, A. and Dave, T. and Debbio, L. Del and Demory, K. and Djukanovic, D. and Draper, T. and Driutti, A. and Endo, M. and Erben, F. and Ferraby, K. and Finkenrath, J. and Flower, L. and Francis, A. and Gámiz, E. and Gogniat, J. and Gündogdu, S. and Hansen, M. T. and Hashimoto, S. and Hayashii, H. and Hertzog, D. W. and Heuser, L. A. and Hostetler, L. and Hou, X. T. and Huang, G. S. and Iijima, T. and Inami, K. and Jüttner, A. and Kitano, R. and Knecht, M. and Kollatzsch, S. and Kronfeld, A. S. and Lenz, T. and Levati, G. and Li, Q. M. and Liao, Y. P. and Libby, J. and Liu, K. F. and Lubicz, V. and Lynch, M. T. and Lytle, A. T. and Ma, J. L. and Miura, K. and Möhling, K. and Muskalla, J. and Noël, F. and Ottnad, K. and Paradisi, P. and Peterson, C. T. and Pitelis, S. and Plura, S. and Price, A. and Radic, D. and Radzhabov, A. and Risch, A. and Romiti, S. and Sahoo, S. and Sannino, F. and Schäfer, H. and Schelhaas, Y. and Serednyakov, S. I. and Shekhovtsova, O. and Simone, J. N. and Simula, S. and Solodov, E. P. and Stokes, F. M. and Vanderhaeghen, M. and Vaquero, A. and Vestergaard, N. and Wang, W. and Yamashita, K. and Yang, Y. B. and Yoshioka, T. and Yuan, C. Z. and Zhevlakov, A. S.},
  date        = {2025-05-27},
  eprint      = {2505.21476},
  eprinttype  = {arXiv},
  eprintclass = {hep-ph},
  doi         = {10.48550/arXiv.2505.21476},
  url         = {http://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21476},
  urldate     = {2025-06-18},
  abstract    = {We present the current Standard Model (SM) prediction for the muon anomalous magnetic moment, \$a\_\textbackslash mu\$, updating the first White Paper (WP20) [1]. The pure QED and electroweak contributions have been further consolidated, while hadronic contributions continue to be responsible for the bulk of the uncertainty of the SM prediction. Significant progress has been achieved in the hadronic light-by-light scattering contribution using both the data-driven dispersive approach as well as lattice-QCD calculations, leading to a reduction of the uncertainty by almost a factor of two. The most important development since WP20 is the change in the estimate of the leading-order hadronic-vacuum-polarization (LO HVP) contribution. A new measurement of the \$e\textasciicircum +e\textasciicircum -\textbackslash to\textbackslash pi\textasciicircum +\textbackslash pi\textasciicircum -\$ cross section by CMD-3 has increased the tensions among data-driven dispersive evaluations of the LO HVP contribution to a level that makes it impossible to combine the results in a meaningful way. At the same time, the attainable precision of lattice-QCD calculations has increased substantially and allows for a consolidated lattice-QCD average of the LO HVP contribution with a precision of about 0.9\textbackslash\%. Adopting the latter in this update has resulted in a major upward shift of the total SM prediction, which now reads \$a\_\textbackslash mu\textasciicircum\textbackslash text\{SM\} = 116\textbackslash,592\textbackslash,033(62)\textbackslash times 10\textasciicircum\{-11\}\$ (530 ppb). When compared against the current experimental average based on the E821 experiment and runs 1-3 of E989 at Fermilab, one finds \$a\_\textbackslash mu\textasciicircum\textbackslash text\{exp\} - a\_\textbackslash mu\textasciicircum\textbackslash text\{SM\} =26(66)\textbackslash times 10\textasciicircum\{-11\}\$, which implies that there is no tension between the SM and experiment at the current level of precision. The final precision of E989 is expected to be around 140 ppb, which is the target of future efforts by the Theory Initiative. The resolution of the tensions among data-driven dispersive evaluations of the LO HVP contribution will be a key element in this endeavor.},
  pubstate    = {prepublished},
  keywords    = {High Energy Physics - Experiment,High Energy Physics - Lattice,High Energy Physics - Phenomenology,Nuclear Experiment,Nuclear Theory},
  note        = {Comment: 188 pages, 83 figures},
